=pod
{- OpenSSL::safe::output_do_not_edit_headers(); -}

=head1 NAME

openssl-generate_tai_chain - decorate a certificate chain with trust anchor
properties

=head1 SYNOPSIS

B<openssl> B<generate_tai_chain>
[B<-help>]
B<-chain> I<filename>
B<-oid> I<id>
[B<-group> I<patterns>]
[B<-trust-anchor-negotiation>]
[B<-out> I<filename>]

=head1 DESCRIPTION

This command reads a PEM certificate chain and writes it back out preceded by a
B<CERTIFICATE PROPERTIES> block that associates a trust anchor identifier with
the chain. When the decorated chain is loaded as a trust anchor, a TLS client
requests that trust anchor by default in the B<trust_anchors> extension, and a
server can select the chain in response (see
L<https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-tls-trust-anchor-ids/>).

The input must not already contain a B<CERTIFICATE PROPERTIES> block, and,
as section 7.4 of the draft requires, must omit the trust anchor itself. The
certificates are copied through unchanged; only the properties block is added.

=head1 OPTIONS

=over 4

=item B<-help>

Print out a usage message.

=item B<-chain> I<filename>

The PEM certificate chain to decorate. This option is required.

=item B<-oid> I<id>

The trust anchor ID, written as a dotted relative object identifier, for
example C<32473.1>. It is emitted as the C<trust_anchor_id> property. This
option is required.

=item B<-group> I<patterns>

A comma-separated list of trust anchor ID patterns, emitted as the
C<trust_anchor_groups> property. Each pattern is written in its text form:
dot-separated components, each a decimal number I<n>, a range
C<{>I<min>C<E<45>>I<max>C<}>, or a range with no upper bound
C<{>I<min>C<E<45>}>. For example,
C<32473.1.2.{0E<45>}.{0E<45>},32473.{123-456}.{789E<45>}>.

=item B<-trust-anchor-negotiation>

Also add the C<trust_anchor_negotiation> property, marking the chain to be
served only when the relying party requested its trust anchor. Off by default.

=item B<-out> I<filename>

The file to write the decorated chain to. Standard output is used if this
option is not given.

=back

=head1 EXAMPLES

Associate the trust anchor ID C<32473.1> with a CA certificate:

 openssl generate_tai_chain -chain ca.pem -oid 32473.1 -out ca-tai.pem

=head1 SEE ALSO

L<openssl(1)>,
L<openssl-verify(1)>,
L<SSL_CTX_set1_requested_trust_anchors(3)>

=head1 HISTORY

This command was added in OpenSSL 4.1.

